  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by an Impulse",
    "lesson_core_move": "Reduce a product $f(t)$$\\delta$(t) to $f(0)$$\\delta$(t) by recognizing that the impulse keeps only the function value at $t = 0$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by an Impulse",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Multiplying a Function by an Impulse",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The impulse $\\delta(t)$ acts like an ideal point sampler at $t=0$. When it multiplies a function, only the function value at that point survives.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(t)\\delta(t)=f(0)\\delta(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So to simplify a product such as $\\left(2t+3\\right)\\delta(t)$, replace the multiplier by its value at $t=0$ and keep the impulse factor. Here, $f(0)=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left(2t+3\\right)\\delta(t)=3\\delta(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same rule also handles a zero value at the impulse location. If the multiplier evaluates to $0$ at $t=0$, then the whole product disappears.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t\\delta(t)=0\\delta(t)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the only mechanism needed here: later products with constants, polynomials, and other symbolic factors all reduce the same way by sampling the multiplier at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by an Impulse",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating $f(0)$ in a Product $f(t)$$\\delta$(t)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Simplify $\\left(4t+1\\right)\\delta(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a multiplier attached to $\\delta(t)$. Use the sampling rule from the previous step: replace the multiplier by its value at $t=0$, then keep the impulse factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(t)=4t+1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now evaluate the multiplier at $t=0$. For this function, $f(0)=4\\cdot 0+1=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(0)=4\\cdot 0+1=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute that sampled value back into the product and simplify the impulse term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left(4t+1\\right)\\delta(t)=f(0)\\delta(t)=1\\delta(t)=\\delta(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the product collapses to $\\delta(t)$ because the impulse keeps only the coefficient at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by an Impulse",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Simplifying Algebraic Factors Multiplying $\\delta$(t)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Simplify $(2t-3)(t+5)\\delta(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a factored algebraic multiplier attached to $\\delta(t)$. Use the same sampling rule: evaluate each factor at $t=0$, then combine the resulting constants before keeping the impulse factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(t)=(2t-3)(t+5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now sample each factor at $t=0$. The first factor becomes $2\\cdot 0-3=-3$, and the second factor becomes $0+5=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(0)=(2\\cdot 0-3)(0+5)=(-3)(5)=-15",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the sampled coefficient back into the impulse product and simplify the result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(2t-3)(t+5)\\delta(t)=f(0)\\delta(t)=-15\\delta(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the factored multiplier collapses to $-15\\delta(t)$ because the impulse keeps only the value of the product at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by an Impulse",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Simplifying Exponential and Sinusoidal Factors Multiplying $\\delta$(t)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Simplify $5e^{-2t}\\sin(\\pi t)\\delta(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Recognize the cue: a factor is multiplying $\\delta(t)$, so the rule is to sample that factor at $t=0$ instead of expanding or integrating it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(t)=5e^{-2t}\\sin(\\pi t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set $f(t)=5e^{-2t}\\sin(\\pi t)$ and evaluate at $t=0$. The exponential becomes $e^0$ and the sine term becomes $\\sin(0)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(0)=5\\cdot e^0\\cdot \\sin(0)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the sampled value back into the impulse product: $5e^{-2t}\\sin(\\pi t)\\delta(t)=f(0)\\delta(t)=0\\delta(t)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5e^{-2t}\\sin(\\pi t)\\delta(t)=f(0)\\delta(t)=0\\delta(t)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The impulse keeps only the value at $t=0$, and here that value is zero, so the entire product collapses to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by an Impulse",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Zero-Weight Products with $\\delta$(t)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Simplify $t(2t+1)(t-4)\\delta(t)$ and decide whether the result is zero.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Recognize the cue: the multiplier contains an explicit $t$ factor, so the impulse rule says to evaluate the whole multiplier at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(t)=t(2t+1)(t-4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compute the sampled value at $t=0$. The first factor is $0$, while the other factors become $1$ and $-4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(0)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot (-4)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the result back into the impulse product: $t(2t+1)(t-4)\\delta(t)=f(0)\\delta(t)=0\\delta(t)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t(2t+1)(t-4)\\delta(t)=f(0)\\delta(t)=0\\delta(t)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The product is zero because the impulse keeps only the value at $t=0$, and any explicit $t$ factor makes that sampled value vanish.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
